  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe main forecast concern will continue to be the well above normal \ntemperatures which will finish out the weekend and continue through \nnext week. This will come courtesy of an upper level ridge which \nwill be anchored over the area. Today through Tuesday the upper \nlevel ridge axis will remain directly over the area. This coupled \nwith moderate WAA over the area through that same period will keep \nhighs 10 to 15 degrees above normal in the mid 90s to near 100. \nThere will be a very gradual increase of dewpoints through this \nperiod which will aid in a gradual increase of heat indices through \nthis period as well. Heat index values today will be in the 95-102 \nrange but will rise to 100-105 by Tuesday. The only shot of \nprecipitation through this period will come this morning across far \nnorthern Missouri where a slight chance (15-25%) of a light \nshower/isolated thunderstorm is possible.\n\nWednesday, the upper level ridge axis begins to slide southeastward \nhowever, the local area will remain under its influence keeping \nhighs in the mid 90s to near 100. Wednesday night into Thursday a \nupper level shortwave will move from the Great Basin region into the \nnorthern Plains. This will force a cold front into the Upper Midwest \nto central Plains. This will increase moisture into the area and \nthis coupled with weak shortwaves rounding the upper level ridge \nwill have the chance (15-25%) to bring a few rounds of storms to the \narea Thursday and Friday. While there is the chance that showers \nand cloud cover may limit temperatures a bit, highs Thursday and \nFriday are still expected to reach the mid to upper 90s. With more \nample moisture being ushered into the area in the Wednesday-Friday \nperiod, heat index values for most will still remain between 100-105 \nhowever, some areas south of the Missouri River may rise into that \n105-110 range which may require heat headlines for part of the \nforecast area. Saturday, models suggest the upper level ridge begins \nto retrograde back west with the axis building back across the \nregion. This will continue to keep temperatures will above normal in \nthe 90s.",
